1️⃣ Kernel — the heart of Linux ❤️


===================================================================
What the kernel is
===================================================================

The kernel is the core of the operating system.
It directly talks to the hardware and manages everything.

👉 Users and applications never talk to hardware directly — only the kernel does.


===================================================================
What the kernel does
===================================================================

🔹 Process management

Creates, schedules, and kills processes
Decides which process gets CPU
Handles multitasking

Examples:
fork()
exec()
kill()


🔹 Memory management

Allocates RAM to processes. 
Handles virtual memory, paging, swapping.
Prevents one process from accessing another’s memory.


🔹 Device management

Talks to hardware via device drivers
Treats devices as files:

/dev/sda
/dev/null
/dev/tty


🔹 File system management

Manages files, directories, permissions
Supports multiple file systems:
ext4, xfs, btrfs, nfs


🔹 System calls

Applications request kernel services using system calls:
read(), write(), open(), close()


**Final :**
The Linux kernel is the core component that manages CPU, memory, devices, filesystems, and provides system calls for applications.
